-## Decisions on a future single synthetic occurrence
-1. Different original outcome/success: preserve it and stop. If the 503 is stub/validation rather than deadline, route to the existing exact g1 category. No backfilled historical cause.
-2. Failed M join: diagnosis unresolved. Remedy only the named measurement failure if a neutral control independently reproduces it. M before stub call: narrow to request read/qualification at reported site. No QA attribution. Operation-timer versus lease-event describes initiating callback only; no clock extension or reset. A lease event alone gives no inbound/root attribution.
-3. M entered stub with no normal response return; QA UNKNOWN: distinguish neither non-delivery nor QA work. Select one benign mount/dispatch vs existing GB1 comparison only if there is a concrete untested wiring difference. Do not modify business routing from silence.
-4. Same M cut plus observed QA entry: delivery to the nominated wrapper occurred by the later read. Receiver not observed: inspect wrapper-to-receiver/validation boundary. QA currently entered pair/timeline, resolution, limit, preparation readback or final check: isolate only that named source interval and its existing child/parent budgets in a neutral control. A later QA catch/return narrows the receiver path whose outcome must be characterized. A later normal form/fulfilled wrapper with M still awaiting stub at cut selects return handoff/lifetime as a comparison candidate; QA may itself have finished after M, so no proof of lost return. Do not tune deadlines or mutate storage.
-5. M stub returned and response reading incomplete: focus on M reader/validation interval; do not blame delivery or assume the QA work caused a timeout. If the frozen record reports a body return or validation transition, inspect intervening checks and the original error/race ordering. A logically impossible timer during contiguous synchronous validation disqualifies the mapping. A valid independent reproduction of exactly one suspected business defect is grounds for a Root repair proposal only, never implementation or authority for a sixth fix.
-6. Missing, poisoned or late data never supplies evidence at the M cut. No unchanged full20 replay. No receipt authentication, provider cause, physical cancellation/settlement, global drain or deployed readiness inference.
